            "Lean philosophy classifies every single operational activity within a manufacturing enterprise into one of three strict categories. The first category is Value-Added (VA) work. An activity is rigorously classified as value-added if and only if it satisfies three inflexible criteria: first, the activity must physically transform the shape, form, chemical composition, or function of the product toward its finished state; second, the customer must be genuinely willing to pay for the activity; and third, the activity must be performed correctly the first first time. Machining a casting, welding structural tubing, blending pharmaceutical powders, and painting body panels are pure value-added activities. In typical un-lean industrial plants, true value-added work accounts for less than five percent of total operational activity.",
            "The second category is Non-Value-Added but Necessary (Type I Muda, or Business Non-Value-Added). These are activities that create zero direct value for the end customer, but are currently mandatory due to regulatory compliance, legal mandates, customer contracts, or the limitations of current machine technology. Examples encompass statutory batch release testing, serialized traceability barcode printing, financial tax invoicing, and calibration of coordinate measuring machines. The Lean objective for Type I waste is not immediate elimination, but aggressive streamlining, automation, and cycle-time compression.",
