            "At the individual project and problem-solving level, Lean Six Sigma standardizes all communication and root-cause analysis using the A3 Problem-Solving Report. Pioneered by Toyota and named after the international standard 11x17-inch paper size, an A3 report forces rigorous, concise, and deeply visual problem-solving onto a single sheet of paper. An A3 is structured into seven logical quadrants mirroring the DMAIC (Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control) and PDCA (Plan, Do, Check, Act) cycles: Box 1 establishes the Strategic Background and Business Case; Box 2 defines the Current Problem Statement and baseline gap; Box 3 sets specific, measurable Target Conditions; Box 4 conducts Root Cause Analysis using Fishbone diagrams and 5-Why analysis; Box 5 outlines Countermeasures and implementation responsibilities; Box 6 tracks the Execution and Verification Plan; and Box 7 establishes Standard Work and Follow-Up governance.",
